The problem asks us to simulate a process involving $N$ aliens.
Each alien $i$ (where $1 \le i \le N$) starts with $A_i$ stones.
Alien $i$ becomes an adult exactly $i$ years later.
When an alien becomes an adult, every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone to the newly adult alien.
We need to find the number of stones each alien has after $N$ years.
Initially, all aliens are minors.
After $N$ years, aliens $1, 2, \ldots, N$ will have become adults at times $1, 2, \ldots, N$ respectively.
Wait, let's re-read carefully.
"The $i$-th alien currently has $A_i$ stones, and will become an adult exactly $i$ years later."
"When someone becomes an adult on this planet, every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone as a congratulatory gift to the alien who has just become an adult."
"Find how many stones each alien will have after $N$ years."

Let's trace the timeline.
At time $t=0$, aliens have $A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_N$ stones.
At time $t=1$, Alien 1 becomes an adult.
At time $t=2$, Alien 2 becomes an adult.
...
At time $t=N$, Alien $N$ becomes an adult.

The condition "every adult who has at least one stone" refers to the set of aliens who are currently adults.
Who are the adults at time $t$?
Alien $i$ becomes an adult at time $i$. So at time $t$, aliens $1, 2, \ldots, t$ are adults.
When Alien $k$ becomes an adult (at time $k$), the set of existing adults is $\{1, 2, \ldots, k-1\}$.
Wait, does the newly adult alien count as an adult *immediately* for the purpose of giving stones?
The problem says "every adult who has at least one stone gives ... to the alien who has just become an adult".
Usually, "just become an adult" implies the event happens at that moment. The newly adult alien receives stones. Does the newly adult alien give stones?
The phrasing "every adult who has at least one stone gives ... to the alien who has just become an adult" suggests the givers are the *already* existing adults. The newly adult is the receiver.
Let's check the sample cases to confirm.

Sample 1:
$N=4$, $A = [5, 0, 9, 3]$.
Initially: $C_1=5, C_2=0, C_3=9, C_4=3$. Adults: None.

Time 1: Alien 1 becomes adult.
Existing adults: None.
So Alien 1 receives 0 stones.
$C_1 = 5$.
Adults: $\{1\}$.

Time 2: Alien 2 becomes adult.
Existing adults: $\{1\}$.
Alien 1 has $C_1=5 \ge 1$. So Alien 1 gives 1 stone to Alien 2.
$C_1$ becomes $5-1=4$.
$C_2$ becomes $0+1=1$.
Adults: $\{1, 2\}$.

Time 3: Alien 3 becomes adult.
Existing adults: $\{1, 2\}$.
Alien 1 has $C_1=4 \ge 1$. Gives 1 stone.
Alien 2 has $C_2=1 \ge 1$. Gives 1 stone.
$C_1$ becomes $4-1=3$.
$C_2$ becomes $1-1=0$.
$C_3$ becomes $9+1+1=11$.
Adults: $\{1, 2, 3\}$.

Time 4: Alien 4 becomes adult.
Existing adults: $\{1, 2, 3\}$.
Alien 1 has $C_1=3 \ge 1$. Gives 1 stone.
Alien 2 has $C_2=0 < 1$. Gives 0 stones.
Alien 3 has $C_3=11 \ge 1$. Gives 1 stone.
$C_1$ becomes $3-1=2$.
$C_2$ remains $0$.
$C_3$ becomes $11-1=1
